    "reading": {
        "body": "Lambda's 31.9% healthy rate over 91 observations separates it from every other provider in this dataset, where the next-lowest figure is OpenAI at 95.9%.\n\nEleven of the twelve providers recorded healthy rates between 95.9% and 100%, a band narrow enough to suggest the industry norm for API availability has settled well above 95%. Five providers, AssemblyAI, Cohere, Deepgram, Groq, and Stability AI, reached 100% across their observation windows. The gap between that cluster and Lambda is not marginal; it is a difference of kind, not degree.\n\nFor workloads that depend on inference APIs, reliability at this layer is a direct constraint on what compute can deliver. A data center full of accelerators produces nothing useful if the API sitting in front of it is unreachable two days out of three. Lambda's figure suggests an infrastructure or operational problem serious enough to affect the majority of sampled intervals, though the data does not identify whether the cause is power, networking, software, or something else.\n\nWhat the data does not cover: observation counts run from 91 to 97, spanning roughly two and a half weeks from a single start date, so this is a short window. Latency, error rates within healthy intervals, and geographic variation are outside scope. One provider, Lambda, had fewer observations than the others, which may itself reflect periods when the monitoring system could not reach the endpoint at all.",
